<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lee de Forest</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1873">1873</a>–<a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lee de Forest was an American inventor and electrical engineer who in 1908 invented the three-element Audion triode vacuum tube, the first practical electronic amplifier. Born in Council Bluffs, Iowa, he earned a doctorate at Yale in 1899. The Audion helped start the Electronic Age and made radio broadcasting and long-distance <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> lines possible; from 1921 he worked on Phonofilm, an optical sound-on-film process. He held over 300 patents, spent much of his income on patent lawsuits and was tried for mail fraud and acquitted. He received the 1922 IRE Medal of Honor and died in Hollywood in 1961.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lee-de-forest.jpg" alt="Lee de Forest" width="245" height="341" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Thbusch at English Wikipedia. Later versions were uploaded by Neutrality at en.wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lee_De_Forest.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lee de Forest was the son of Anna Margaret Robbins and Henry Swift DeForest, a Congregational minister who hoped his son would become a pastor. In 1879 the elder de Forest became president of Talladega College in Talladega, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, which educated mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a>. Many local white citizens resented the school, and Lee spent most of his youth isolated from the white community, with several close friends among the Black children of the town. Donald Beaver traces his independence and inventiveness to his intense desire to overcome the deficiencies of his childhood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Convinced that wireless telegraphy had a great future, de Forest set out to improve on Marconi's system, whose coherer receiver was slow, insensitive and unreliable. In January 1902 he met the promoter Abraham White, who became his main sponsor for five years; White formed the American DeForest Wireless Telegraph Company, but the enterprise rested on exaggeration and stock fraud. De Forest transmitted audio across a room with his arc transmitter on 31 December 1906, and in 1908 music was broadcast from the Eiffel Tower during his honeymoon.</p><p>In March 1912 he and four other officials of the Radio Telephone Company were arrested for using the mails to defraud. At the trials in late 1913 three were found guilty and de Forest was acquitted. In October 1914 he sold the Audion's commercial radio rights to AT&amp;T for $90,000. He was in a bitter priority dispute with Edwin Howard Armstrong over regeneration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The grid Audion, the first successful triode and the first device that could amplify electrical signals, was his best-known invention. He traced its inspiration to 1900, when he thought a flickering gas flame responded to electromagnetic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulses">pulses</a> from a spark-gap transmitter; he soon found the cause was air-pressure changes from the spark. At Federal Telegraph he chained up to three Audions in a cascade amplifier. The Audion and the tubes developed from it dominated electronics for 40 years, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transistor">transistor</a> of 1948 led to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/integrated-circuit">microchips</a>.</p><p>In 1921 he turned from radio to Phonofilm, an optical sound-on-film process that recorded a microphone's electrical waveforms photographically and played them back in step with the picture. He received the 1922 IRE Medal of Honor, the 1946 Edison Medal and an honorary Academy Award in 1960. A crater on the far side of the Moon is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lee de Forest” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
